const __vite_ssr_export_default__ = /* #__PURE__ */ JSON.parse("[\n  {\n    \"day\": 1,\n    \"title\": \"Meet Dukaan: your first program\",\n    \"intro\": \"In this course you will build one real thing: Dukaan, a shop management system. Today it is a few lines that list stock on a screen. Step by step it will grow — a database, an API, a website, a live deployment, AI features. At the end you will demo a complete system in interviews and prove you can build software from scratch. It all starts here, with lesson 1: a program is instructions plus data.\",\n    \"objectives\": [\n      \"Explain what a program is in one sentence: instructions plus data.\",\n      \"Run a Python program and describe what each line does.\",\n      \"Change one value, predict the new output, and check your prediction.\"\n    ],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"You are building Dukaan\",\n        \"text\": \"Here is the whole journey, so you always know where you are. Days 1-7: Dukaan is a command-line stock list. Days 8-14: it gets version control and tests. Days 15-21: it gets AI features, like a chatbot that answers stock questions. Days 22-30: it becomes a real API on the internet. Days 31-90: a database, a website, logins, deployment on cloud servers. Each step happens because the shop outgrows the last version — that is how you will learn architecture: by feeling why each piece is needed.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"A program is instructions plus data\",\n        \"text\": \"Data is the facts: the shop name, the item names, the prices. Instructions are what to do with the facts: remember this value, show that line on the screen. Code is just these two things written down. When you run the program, Python reads your instructions from top to bottom and carries them out. Everything you will ever build — websites, APIs, AI systems — is instructions plus data, stacked higher.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Your three tools\",\n        \"text\": \"You need three tools, and they do three different jobs. The editor (VS Code) is where you write the instructions. Python is the runner — it reads your file and carries out the instructions. The terminal is the messenger — you type 'python dukaan.py' there, which means 'hey computer, run this file with Python.' Never type terminal commands inside your Python file, and never paste Python code into the terminal as a command. Three tools, three jobs.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Predict before you run\",\n        \"text\": \"Here is the habit that separates engineers from typists: before you run a changed program, say out loud what you expect to see. Then run it. If you were right, you understand it. If you were wrong, you just found something to learn — read the lines in order until you see why. Do this every time, starting today.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"example\": {\n      \"language\": \"python\",\n      \"filename\": \"dukaan.py\",\n      \"run\": \"Run python dukaan.py from the starter directory.\",\n      \"context\": \"Amina's shop needs a stock list on screen. Three items, three prices. Read the code, predict the exact output, then run it.\",\n      \"code\": \"shop_name = \\\"Amina Store\\\"\\nitem1 = \\\"Rice\\\"\\nprice1 = 250\\nitem2 = \\\"Sugar\\\"\\nprice2 = 180\\nitem3 = \\\"Tea\\\"\\nprice3 = 950\\n\\nprint(f\\\"Welcome to {shop_name}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"1. {item1} - Rs {price1}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"2. {item2} - Rs {price2}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"3. {item3} - Rs {price3}\\\")\",\n      \"output\": \"Welcome to Amina Store\\n1. Rice - Rs 250\\n2. Sugar - Rs 180\\n3. Tea - Rs 950\",\n      \"walkthrough\": \"The first seven lines store data: one shop name, three item names, three prices. Text goes inside quotation marks; prices are plain numbers. The last four lines are instructions: print shows text on the screen, and the f before the quotation marks lets the stored values appear inside the text. Python runs top to bottom, so the welcome line prints first. This is Dukaan v0.1 — the seed of everything you will build.\"\n    },\n    \"exercises\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Change the shop\",\n        \"task\": \"Change the shop name to your own shop name and change one price. First write down exactly what the output will look like. Then run it.\",\n        \"hint\": \"Only change the values on the right side of the = sign.\",\n        \"check\": \"The output matches your written prediction exactly.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Add an item\",\n        \"task\": \"Add a fourth item with its price, and one more print line so it appears in the list.\",\n        \"hint\": \"Copy the pattern: one line to store the name, one line to store the price, one print line to show it.\",\n        \"check\": \"Four items print, each with the correct price.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Break it on purpose\",\n        \"task\": \"Delete one quotation mark and run the program. Read the error message carefully — which line does it blame? Then fix it.\",\n        \"hint\": \"Python tells you the line number. The real mistake is often just before it.\",\n        \"check\": \"You can point at the error and explain in your own words what went wrong.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"mistakes\": [\n      \"Typing 'python dukaan.py' inside the Python file — that is a terminal command, not a Python instruction.\",\n      \"Forgetting quotation marks around text — Python then thinks your words are instruction names.\",\n      \"Changing three things at once when something breaks — change one thing, predict, run, observe.\"\n    ],\n    \"fde\": \"In a software house, a junior's first tickets look exactly like this: small, clear, testable. 'Show the stock list on screen.' Senior engineers trust juniors who write small programs they can explain line by line. That is what you practiced today — the atom of the job. Every system you will ever build is made of atoms like this one.\",\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"Dukaan v0.1 — the stock list\",\n      \"brief\": \"Amina runs a small shop. She keeps stock in her head and on paper slips. She wants a program that shows her stock list on screen: item names and prices, clear enough to read at a glance. Build version 0.1 — it only displays the list. This is the first brick of the system you will grow for the whole course.\",\n      \"deliverables\": [\n        \"A dukaan.py file that runs with python dukaan.py and shows the stock list.\",\n        \"The program's output, copied into your notes or a screenshot.\",\n        \"A two-line README: what the program does and how to run it.\"\n      ],\n      \"milestones\": [\n        {\n          \"title\": \"Show the shop name\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Create dukaan.py in your editor.\",\n            \"Store the shop name and print a welcome line.\",\n            \"Run it from the terminal.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"The welcome line prints with your shop name.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"title\": \"List three items\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Store three item names and three prices.\",\n            \"Print each item on its own line with its price.\",\n            \"Run it and read the full list.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"All three items show with the correct prices.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"title\": \"Change it and predict\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Change one price.\",\n            \"Write down what you expect the output to be.\",\n            \"Run it and compare.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"Your prediction matches the real output.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"acceptance\": [\n        \"python dukaan.py runs with no errors.\",\n        \"The shop name appears on the first line.\",\n        \"Three items appear, each with the correct price.\",\n        \"Changing a price changes only that line in the output.\",\n        \"You can explain every line to another person without reading.\"\n      ],\n      \"tests\": [\n        [\n          \"Run with the starter values\",\n          \"Welcome to Amina Store, then 3 items priced 250, 180, 950\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Change price1 to 300 and run\",\n          \"The Rice line shows Rs 300; the other lines are unchanged\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Change shop_name and run\",\n          \"The first line shows the new shop name\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Delete one quotation mark and run\",\n          \"Python shows an error naming the line (then fix it and re-run)\"\n        ]\n      ],\n      \"extensions\": [\n        \"Ask the shopkeeper: use input() to ask for the shop name when the program starts.\",\n        \"Add a total: print the sum of all three prices as the last line.\"\n      ],\n      \"review\": [\n        \"Can you explain each line without looking at the screen?\",\n        \"Did you predict the output before running, at least twice?\",\n        \"To add a fourth item, what would you change — and how many minutes would it take?\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"references\": [\n      [\n        \"Python: using the interpreter\",\n        \"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/interpreter.html\"\n      ]\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 2,\n    \"title\": \"The price list\",\n    \"intro\": \"Dukaan v0.1 shows the stock list. Today it grows into v0.2: a bill calculator. A customer buys 3 bags of rice and 2 of sugar - the program multiplies, adds, and works out the change. The new idea is small, and getting it wrong breaks everything: text and numbers are different things, and the shopkeeper's typed input always arrives as text. Convert it, or the bill is nonsense.\",\n    \"objectives\": [\n      \"Explain the difference between text and numbers with a shop example.\",\n      \"Use *, +, and - to compute a bill total and the change.\",\n      \"Convert typed input from text to a number with int() before doing arithmetic.\"\n    ],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Text and numbers look the same but behave differently\",\n        \"text\": \"The price 250 and the text \\\"250\\\" look identical on your screen. To Python they are different things. 250 + 180 is 430 - arithmetic. \\\"250\\\" + \\\"180\\\" is \\\"250180\\\" - gluing text together. Text sticks; numbers calculate. A price you want to multiply must be a number, not text. Python calls text str (short for string, like a string of characters) and whole numbers int (short for integer). When output looks wrong - a total of 250180 instead of 430 - your first suspect is always: text where a number should be.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Bills are arithmetic\",\n        \"text\": \"A bill is three operations. Multiply: price times quantity for each item. Add: the line totals into a grand total. Subtract: money paid minus the total gives the change. Python writes them as *, +, and -. Order matters: Python multiplies before it adds, exactly like the maths you learned in school, so 250 * 3 + 180 * 2 means (250 * 3) + (180 * 2). When in doubt, add brackets - they cost nothing and prevent real money mistakes.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"input() always gives you text\",\n        \"text\": \"When a program asks the shopkeeper to type a quantity, the answer arrives as text - even if they typed digits. \\\"3\\\" is text, and \\\"3\\\" * 2 is \\\"33\\\", not 6. So the first thing you do with typed input is convert it: int(\\\"3\\\") turns the text into the number 3, and then you can multiply. If the text is not a number - int(\\\"three\\\") - Python stops and shows an error naming the line. That error is useful information, not a disaster: it tells you exactly what went wrong. On Day 7 you will learn to catch it gracefully instead of crashing.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"example\": {\n      \"language\": \"python\",\n      \"filename\": \"bill.py\",\n      \"run\": \"Run python bill.py from the starter directory.\",\n      \"context\": \"Amina's customer buys rice and sugar. The quantities were typed by the shopkeeper, so they arrive as text. Read the code, predict the exact output - especially the change - then run it.\",\n      \"code\": \"price_rice = 250\\nprice_sugar = 180\\n\\ntyped_rice_qty = \\\"3\\\"\\ntyped_sugar_qty = \\\"2\\\"\\n\\nqty_rice = int(typed_rice_qty)\\nqty_sugar = int(typed_sugar_qty)\\n\\nrice_total = price_rice * qty_rice\\nsugar_total = price_sugar * qty_sugar\\ntotal = rice_total + sugar_total\\n\\npaid = 2000\\nchange = paid - total\\n\\nprint(f\\\"Rice: {qty_rice} x Rs {price_rice} = Rs {rice_total}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Sugar: {qty_sugar} x Rs {price_sugar} = Rs {sugar_total}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Total: Rs {total}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Paid Rs {paid}, change Rs {change}\\\")\",\n      \"output\": \"Rice: 3 x Rs 250 = Rs 750\\nSugar: 2 x Rs 180 = Rs 360\\nTotal: Rs 1110\\nPaid Rs 2000, change Rs 890\",\n      \"walkthrough\": \"The first two lines store prices as numbers. The next two store the typed quantities as text - this is what input() really gives you. int() converts each one into a real number. The line totals are price times quantity; adding them gives the grand total, 1110. Change is paid minus total: 2000 - 1110 = 890. If you skip the int() conversion, \\\"3\\\" * 250 does not multiply - it repeats the text \\\"3\\\" two hundred and fifty times. That is why conversion comes before arithmetic, every time.\"\n    },\n    \"exercises\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Predict the trap\",\n        \"task\": \"In a scratch file, run print(\\\"2\\\" + \\\"2\\\") and print(2 + 2). Before each one, write down what you expect to see.\",\n        \"hint\": \"Text sticks together; numbers calculate.\",\n        \"check\": \"You predicted \\\"22\\\" for the text version and 4 for the number version before running.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"A tea bill\",\n        \"task\": \"A customer buys 2 packs of Tea (Rs 950 each) and pays Rs 2000. Write the lines that compute the total and the change. Predict both numbers, then run.\",\n        \"hint\": \"Copy the pattern: line total, then paid minus total.\",\n        \"check\": \"Total is Rs 1900 and change is Rs 100, matching your prediction.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Break the conversion\",\n        \"task\": \"Change typed_rice_qty to \\\"three\\\" and run. Read the error: which line does it blame, and what does ValueError mean in your own words? Then change it back.\",\n        \"hint\": \"ValueError means \\\"this value cannot become a number\\\".\",\n        \"check\": \"You can explain why int(\\\"three\\\") fails without re-reading the message.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"mistakes\": [\n      \"Forgetting int() on typed input - then \\\"3\\\" * 250 repeats text instead of multiplying.\",\n      \"Using + on two pieces of text when you meant to add numbers - \\\"250\\\" + \\\"180\\\" is \\\"250180\\\", not 430.\",\n      \"Trying int() on text like \\\"3kg\\\" or \\\"three\\\" - only pure digit text converts; anything else is an error.\"\n    ],\n    \"fde\": \"In a software house, junior tickets are full of this. \\\"Add a totals row to the receipt\\\" is a feature ticket. \\\"The discount shows 250180 instead of 430 - fix it\\\" is a bug ticket, and it is a text-vs-number bug. Seniors trust juniors who look at wrong output and name the cause in ten seconds. Today you earned that instinct: when numbers look glued together, suspect text.\",\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"Dukaan v0.2 - totals and bills\",\n      \"brief\": \"Amina's customers are tired of mental maths. She wants Dukaan to print a proper bill: each item's quantity times price, the grand total, the amount paid, and the change. Quantities come from the shopkeeper typing at the keyboard. Build v0.2 on top of v0.1 - the stock list stays, the bill is new.\",\n      \"deliverables\": [\n        \"dukaan.py extended: after the stock list, it asks for quantities and prints a bill.\",\n        \"The bill shows each line (qty x price), the grand total, the paid amount, and the change.\",\n        \"A note in your own words: why int() was needed, with one example.\"\n      ],\n      \"milestones\": [\n        {\n          \"title\": \"One line total\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Pick one item and a fixed quantity.\",\n            \"Compute price times quantity and print the line.\",\n            \"Predict the number before you run.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"The line total equals price times quantity.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"title\": \"The full bill\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Add a second item's line total.\",\n            \"Add both lines into a grand total and print it.\",\n            \"Compute change from a fixed paid amount and print it.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"Total and change are correct for your test quantities.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"title\": \"Real typed input\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Use input() to ask for each quantity.\",\n            \"Convert the answers with int() before multiplying.\",\n            \"Test with quantities 1, 5, and 0.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"Typing 5 gives five times the price; typing 0 gives Rs 0.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"acceptance\": [\n        \"The program prints the v0.1 stock list unchanged.\",\n        \"It asks for quantities and prints a bill with line totals, a grand total, and change.\",\n        \"Every quantity is converted with int() before any arithmetic.\",\n        \"Quantity 0 gives a line total of Rs 0; quantity 5 gives five times the price.\",\n        \"You can explain why removing int() would break the bill.\"\n      ],\n      \"tests\": [\n        [\n          \"Quantities: rice 3, sugar 2, tea 0; paid 2000\",\n          \"Lines show Rs 750, Rs 360, Rs 0; total Rs 1110; change Rs 890\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Quantity typed as 0 for every item\",\n          \"Total Rs 0; change equals the paid amount\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Quantity typed as \\\"two\\\"\",\n          \"Program shows an error naming the conversion (fix: type digits)\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Change price1 to 300 and re-run the bill\",\n          \"Rice line and total update; nothing else changes\"\n        ]\n      ],\n      \"extensions\": [\n        \"Print a \\\"THANK YOU\\\" footer with the shop name and the total items sold.\",\n        \"Ask for the paid amount with input() too, and print a warning if paid is less than the total.\"\n      ],\n      \"review\": [\n        \"Where exactly does text become a number in your program - and what breaks if you skip it?\",\n        \"Show the bill for 4 bags of rice without running the program. How did you work it out?\",\n        \"What would you change to add a fourth item to the bill?\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"references\": [\n      [\n        \"Python: numbers\",\n        \"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/introduction.html#numbers\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Python: strings\",\n        \"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/introduction.html#strings\"\n      ]\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 3,\n    \"title\": \"The smart stock check\",\n    \"intro\": \"Amina has two morning jobs. One: check every item's stock and flag what is low. Two: apply the Friday rule - bills above Rs 1000 get 10% off. Writing each check by hand means one block per item, and next month there will be thirty items. Today Dukaan v0.3 learns the two halves of real work: decisions (ask a yes/no question) and loops (repeat the check for every item). One loop, one decision inside it, and the whole shop gets checked.\",\n    \"objectives\": [\n      \"Write if/elif/else decisions that apply business rules like discounts and stock alerts.\",\n      \"Use a for loop to run the same check once per item, however many items there are.\",\n      \"Combine a loop with a decision so every item is checked automatically.\"\n    ],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Decisions are yes/no questions\",\n        \"text\": \"A shopkeeper decides all day: is the bill big enough for a discount? Is the stock too low? In code, each decision is a question with two answers - yes or no - written with if. The lines indented under if run only when the answer is yes. The lines under else run when the answer is no. Indentation is not decoration; it is how Python knows which lines belong to the decision. Rules with two questions use and (both must be yes) or or (one yes is enough): \\\"bill above 2000 AND it is Friday\\\" needs both; \\\"tea is low OR sugar is low\\\" needs one.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Comparisons ask, == is not =\",\n        \"text\": \"total > 1000 asks \\\"is the total above 1000?\\\" The answer is True (yes) or False (no). Watch the boundary: with >, a total of exactly 1000 gets no discount - the question is \\\"strictly above\\\". With >=, exactly 1000 would qualify. Real bugs live on boundaries, so always test the exact boundary value. And == (two equals signs) compares; = (one equals sign) stores. Mixing them up is the most common beginner error in every country: \\\"if total = 1000\\\" is always wrong.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Repeating beats copying\",\n        \"text\": \"Without a loop, checking three items means writing the check three times. Thirty items means thirty copies - and the day you fix a bug, you must fix it thirty times. A for loop says: \\\"here is the job; do it once per value.\\\" You write the check once, and Python repeats it for every item. Fewer copies, fewer mistakes, and adding a fourth item changes nothing in the loop.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"for walks through a row of values\",\n        \"text\": \"A list is values in a row inside square brackets: prices = [250, 180, 950]. (You will master lists on Day 5; today just borrow the idea.) \\\"for price in prices:\\\" means \\\"take each value in turn, call it price, and run the block.\\\" First round price is 250, then 180, then 950 - then the loop ends. The block is the indented lines, just like with if. Two more tools for later: range(3) counts 0, 1, 2 for a fixed number of rounds, and while repeats until its question turns false - but a while loop must change its answer inside the block, or it never stops.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"example\": {\n      \"language\": \"python\",\n      \"filename\": \"smartcheck.py\",\n      \"run\": \"Run python smartcheck.py from the starter directory.\",\n      \"context\": \"Amina's two morning jobs in one program: a loop checks every item's stock and flags what is low, then one decision applies the Friday discount to a Rs 1200 bill. Predict every printed line before you run - especially which items get flagged.\",\n      \"code\": \"names = [\\\"Rice\\\", \\\"Sugar\\\", \\\"Tea\\\"]\\nprices = [250, 180, 950]\\nstocks = [40, 90, 25]\\n\\nprint(\\\"Morning stock check:\\\")\\nfor i in range(3):\\n    if stocks[i] < 50:\\n        print(f\\\"{names[i]}: Rs {prices[i]} - LOW STOCK, order more!\\\")\\n    else:\\n        print(f\\\"{names[i]}: Rs {prices[i]} - OK\\\")\\n\\ntotal = 1200\\nif total > 1000:\\n    discount = total / 10\\nelse:\\n    discount = 0\\nprint(f\\\"Bill: Rs {total}, discount: Rs {discount}, to pay: Rs {total - discount}\\\")\",\n      \"output\": \"Morning stock check:\\nRice: Rs 250 - LOW STOCK, order more!\\nSugar: Rs 180 - OK\\nTea: Rs 950 - LOW STOCK, order more!\\nBill: Rs 1200, discount: Rs 120.0, to pay: Rs 1080.0\",\n      \"walkthrough\": \"The loop runs three rounds with i = 0, 1, 2. Round 0: stocks[0] is 40, below 50, so Rice gets the LOW STOCK line. Round 1: stocks[1] is 90, so Sugar gets OK. Round 2: stocks[2] is 25, so Tea is flagged. Then the decision: 1200 > 1000 is True, so discount = 120.0 (division always gives a decimal number: 120.0). To pay is 1200 - 120.0 = 1080.0. Now predict: with total = 1000, the discount question is False - no discount. That boundary is where real discount bugs hide.\"\n    },\n    \"exercises\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Test the boundary\",\n        \"task\": \"Set total to 1000 and predict the discount. Set it to 1001 and predict again. Run both.\",\n        \"hint\": \"The rule says \\\"above 1000\\\", not \\\"1000 or above\\\".\",\n        \"check\": \"Rs 0 discount at 1000, Rs 100.1 at 1001 - both predicted before running.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Count the alerts\",\n        \"task\": \"Add a counter that counts how many items are LOW STOCK, and print the count after the loop.\",\n        \"hint\": \"Create low = 0 before the loop; add 1 inside the LOW STOCK branch.\",\n        \"check\": \"The count is 2 for the starter stocks.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Add a fourth item\",\n        \"task\": \"Add \\\"Flour\\\", 120, 60 to the three lists and change range(3) to range(4). Predict the new lines, then run.\",\n        \"hint\": \"All three lists and the range must agree on 4.\",\n        \"check\": \"Four status lines print; no other code changed.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"mistakes\": [\n      \"Writing = when you mean == - one stores a value, two compares.\",\n      \"Forgetting the colon after the if or for line - Python needs it to start a block.\",\n      \"Wrong indentation - a line that looks inside the block but is not indented runs every time (or never, inside a loop).\",\n      \"range(3) with four items - the loop only visits the first three. The range and the lists must agree.\"\n    ],\n    \"fde\": \"Two of the most common junior tickets arrive together: \\\"apply this business rule\\\" and \\\"do it for every row\\\". Business rules change constantly - \\\"discount now starts at Rs 1500\\\" - and seniors always ask \\\"what happens at exactly the threshold?\\\" Every-row work is always a loop; copy-pasting thirty checks is thirty chances to break. Today you practiced both halves of real tickets: the decision and the repetition.\",\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"Dukaan v0.3 - the smart stock checker\",\n      \"brief\": \"Amina opens the shop each morning with two jobs: check every item's stock, and apply the Friday discount to big bills. Build v0.3: one program that loops over all items printing a status line each (OK or LOW STOCK), reports the cheapest and most expensive item, then applies the discount rule to the bill. The rules live in clearly marked variables at the top, so Amina can change them without touching the logic.\",\n      \"deliverables\": [\n        \"dukaan.py loops over the items - no repeated per-item lines.\",\n        \"Each item prints one status line: name, price, OK or LOW STOCK; the end prints cheapest, priciest, and the discounted bill.\",\n        \"DISCOUNT_LIMIT and LOW_STOCK_LIMIT variables at the top, plus a test note with the boundary values you tried.\"\n      ],\n      \"milestones\": [\n        {\n          \"title\": \"The discount decision\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Add the if/else discount after the bill total.\",\n            \"Test with totals 900, 1000, and 1100.\",\n            \"Write down the boundary result.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"1000 gets no discount; 1100 gets 10% off.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"title\": \"The loop check\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Store names, prices, and stocks in three lists.\",\n            \"Replace per-item lines with one loop that prints a status line each.\",\n            \"Add a fourth item and confirm nothing else changes.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"Four items print from one loop body; the fourth needed no new logic.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"title\": \"Rules Amina can change\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Move the numbers 1000, 10, and 50 into named variables at the top.\",\n            \"Change the discount to start at 1500 and re-run.\",\n            \"Confirm only the variable changed, not the logic.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"One variable change updates the whole program's behavior.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"acceptance\": [\n        \"All items print from a loop - adding an item means adding to the lists, not writing new code.\",\n        \"Every item below the stock limit is flagged LOW STOCK; items at or above print OK.\",\n        \"A bill of exactly Rs 1000 shows no discount; above it shows 10% off.\",\n        \"Changing DISCOUNT_LIMIT to 1500 changes behavior with no other edits.\",\n        \"You can explain what the loop variable holds on each round, and the difference between = and ==.\"\n      ],\n      \"tests\": [\n        [\n          \"Bill total Rs 1100\",\n          \"Discount Rs 110.0 shown; to pay Rs 990.0\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Bill total exactly Rs 1000\",\n          \"No discount; to pay Rs 1000\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Stocks 40, 90, 25 (limit 50)\",\n          \"Rice and Tea flagged LOW; Sugar OK\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Fourth item added (Flour, 120, 60)\",\n          \"Four status lines; no new per-item code\"\n        ]\n      ],\n      \"extensions\": [\n        \"Print the total value of all stock (price x qty added up inside the loop).\",\n        \"Alert differently for empty stock (0): \\\"OUT OF STOCK - urgent!\\\" vs the normal low alert.\"\n      ],\n      \"review\": [\n        \"Why is one loop better than three copied blocks when Amina adds a fourth item?\",\n        \"A customer argues their Rs 1000 bill deserves the discount. What does the code say - and is the rule or the code wrong?\",\n        \"What breaks if the three lists have different lengths? How would you notice?\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"references\": [\n      [\n        \"Python: if statements\",\n        \"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#if-statements\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Python: for statements\",\n        \"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#for-statements\"\n      ]\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"The reusable counter\",\n    \"intro\": \"Dukaan now computes totals, discounts, and stock checks - but the same small jobs are scattered everywhere: \\\"price times quantity\\\" appears in the bill, in the stock value, in the report. When Amina changes a rule, you must hunt down every copy. Today v0.4 gets organized: each repeated job becomes a function - a named, reusable piece with inputs and an answer.\",\n    \"objectives\": [\n      \"Define a function with def that packages one job under one name.\",\n      \"Pass inputs as parameters and get answers back with return.\",\n      \"Explain the difference between printing a value and returning it.\"\n    ],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"A function is a job with a name\",\n        \"text\": \"def total_value(price, qty): creates a reusable job called total_value. The indented lines are the job's instructions - they only run when you call the function by name: total_value(250, 40). You write the logic once and use it ten times. When the rule changes, you change it in one place. That is the whole point: one definition, many uses. Name it after the job it does, not after how it does it.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Parameters in, return out\",\n        \"text\": \"price and qty are parameters - empty slots that get filled each time you call. return sends the answer back to the caller: rice_value = total_value(250, 40) stores 10000 in rice_value. Contrast this with print: print shows a value on the screen and gives back nothing. A function that prints its answer cannot be reused in a calculation - you cannot add up printed lines. Return the answer; let the caller decide whether to print it.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"True and False are answers too\",\n        \"text\": \"is_low_stock(40) answers a yes/no question, so it returns True or False. True and False are Python's words for yes and no - they are values, just like numbers. You can store them, print them, and use them directly in an if: \\\"if is_low_stock(stock):\\\" reads almost like English. Functions that answer questions make decisions readable, and readable code is code the team trusts.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"example\": {\n      \"language\": \"python\",\n      \"filename\": \"counter.py\",\n      \"run\": \"Run python counter.py from the starter directory.\",\n      \"context\": \"Amina wants two reusable jobs: the value of some stock (price times quantity) and a low-stock test. Read the code, predict all four printed lines, then run.\",\n      \"code\": \"def total_value(price, qty):\\n    return price * qty\\n\\ndef is_low_stock(stock):\\n    return stock < 50\\n\\nrice_value = total_value(250, 40)\\nsugar_value = total_value(180, 90)\\n\\nprint(f\\\"Rice stock value: Rs {rice_value}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Sugar stock value: Rs {sugar_value}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Rice low? {is_low_stock(40)}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Sugar low? {is_low_stock(90)}\\\")\",\n      \"output\": \"Rice stock value: Rs 10000\\nSugar stock value: Rs 16200\\nRice low? True\\nSugar low? False\",\n      \"walkthrough\": \"The two def blocks define jobs but run nothing yet - defining is not calling. total_value(250, 40) fills price with 250 and qty with 40, computes 10000, and return hands it back to be stored in rice_value. is_low_stock(40) asks \\\"40 < 50?\\\" - True. is_low_stock(90) asks \\\"90 < 50?\\\" - False. Notice the callers never see inside the functions; they only know the name, the inputs, and the answer. That separation is what lets you reuse code and change it safely.\"\n    },\n    \"exercises\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"A bill-line function\",\n        \"task\": \"Write bill_line(price, qty) that returns price times qty. Call it for (950, 2) and (180, 5). Predict both answers, then run.\",\n        \"hint\": \"One def, one return, two calls.\",\n        \"check\": \"1900 and 900 - both predicted before running.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Return vs print\",\n        \"task\": \"Write a version of total_value that prints the answer instead of returning it. Then try adding two calls together. What happens, and why?\",\n        \"hint\": \"A function with no return gives back a special value called None.\",\n        \"check\": \"You can explain why adding returned values works but adding printed output fails.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Reuse three times\",\n        \"task\": \"Use total_value three times - rice, sugar, tea - and add the three answers into stock_worth. Predict the total, then run.\",\n        \"hint\": \"worth = total_value(250, 40) + total_value(180, 90) + total_value(950, 25)\",\n        \"check\": \"Rs 49950, matching your prediction. One function, three uses.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"mistakes\": [\n      \"Calling a function before defining it - Python reads top to bottom, so def must come first.\",\n      \"Forgetting return - the function then gives back None, and None + 5 is an error.\",\n      \"Printing inside the function instead of returning - then the answer cannot be reused in maths.\"\n    ],\n    \"fde\": \"In code review, seniors constantly say: \\\"extract this into a function - you wrote it three times.\\\" Duplicated logic is how real bugs are born: the discount gets fixed in the bill but not in the report. Juniors who name small jobs well - total_value, is_low_stock - write code the whole team can read. Today's project is your first refactor: same behavior, better shape. That word will follow you for your whole career.\",\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"Dukaan v0.4 - organized code\",\n      \"brief\": \"Dukaan works, but its logic is scattered: price times quantity is computed in three places, the low-stock test in two. Amina wants a small change - the low-stock limit moves to 40 - and you should only have to change it once. Rebuild v0.4: same program, organized into functions. Nothing the user sees changes; everything about the code gets better.\",\n      \"deliverables\": [\n        \"dukaan.py rewritten with functions: line_total, is_low_stock, bill_total at minimum.\",\n        \"No duplicated price-times-quantity logic - every use calls the function.\",\n        \"The low-stock limit appears exactly once in the file.\",\n        \"A before/after note: which lines disappeared and why the new version is easier to change.\"\n      ],\n      \"milestones\": [\n        {\n          \"title\": \"Extract the repeated jobs\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Find every place that computes price times quantity.\",\n            \"Write line_total(price, qty) and replace each copy with a call.\",\n            \"Run the bill and confirm the numbers are unchanged.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"The bill totals are identical to v0.4's.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"title\": \"One home for each rule\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Write is_low_stock(stock) with the limit inside it.\",\n            \"Replace every stock comparison with a call.\",\n            \"Change the limit to 40 in one place and re-run.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"One edit changes every stock check in the program.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"title\": \"Tidy and prove\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Move all def blocks to the top, calls below.\",\n            \"Re-run every test from v0.4.\",\n            \"Write the before/after note.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"All v0.4 tests still pass with the organized code.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"acceptance\": [\n        \"Every price-times-quantity computation goes through line_total - no inline copies remain.\",\n        \"Every low-stock decision goes through is_low_stock - the limit appears once.\",\n        \"The program's output is identical to v0.4 for the same inputs.\",\n        \"Changing the stock limit to 40 requires editing exactly one line.\",\n        \"You can explain the difference between defining a function and calling it.\"\n      ],\n      \"tests\": [\n        [\n          \"Run the v0.4 bill with v0.3 quantities\",\n          \"Identical totals to v0.3\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Change limit to 40; an item with stock 45\",\n          \"No longer flagged (it was flagged at 50)\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Call line_total(950, 0)\",\n          \"Returns 0\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Search the file for price-times-qty outside line_total\",\n          \"No matches - no duplicated logic\"\n        ]\n      ],\n      \"extensions\": [\n        \"Add grand_total(prices, qtys) that loops and calls line_total inside - functions calling functions.\",\n        \"Add cheapest_item(names, prices) returning the name of the cheapest item.\"\n      ],\n      \"review\": [\n        \"What is the difference between defining a function and calling it?\",\n        \"Why is returning better than printing inside a reusable function?\",\n        \"If Amina adds a \\\"damaged goods\\\" discount, which function would change - and which would not?\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"references\": [\n      [\n        \"Python: defining functions\",\n        \"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#defining-functions\"\n      ]\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 5,\n    \"title\": \"The real stock book\",\n    \"intro\": \"Three separate lists - names, prices, quantities - are an accident waiting to happen: delete from one list and forget the others, and rice gets sugar's price. Real programs keep each item's facts together. Today Dukaan v0.5 gets a real stock book: a list of dictionaries, where every item is one labeled bundle. Add, remove, and search - the way real data works.\",\n    \"objectives\": [\n      \"Store ordered values in a list and grow it with append().\",\n      \"Label values with a dictionary so item[\\\"price\\\"] replaces price1.\",\n      \"Build and search a list of dictionaries as a tiny in-memory database.\"\n    ],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"A list keeps things in order\",\n        \"text\": \"A list is values in a row: names = [\\\"Rice\\\", \\\"Sugar\\\", \\\"Tea\\\"]. Positions start at 0: names[0] is \\\"Rice\\\". append() adds to the end, so the list grows without you rewriting it. len() tells you how many values there are. Lists are for \\\"many of the same kind of thing\\\" - many items, many prices, many customers. One row, one idea.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"A dictionary labels each value\",\n        \"text\": \"A dictionary is values with name tags: item = {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Rice\\\", \\\"price\\\": 250, \\\"qty\\\": 40}. You read a value by its label: item[\\\"price\\\"] gives 250. Labels beat positions: item[\\\"price\\\"] still works if you add new labels later, while \\\"the second value\\\" breaks the moment the order changes. Use the wrong label - item[\\\"cost\\\"] - and Python answers with a KeyError: the label does not exist. Label spelling must match exactly, including capitals.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"A list of dictionaries is a tiny database\",\n        \"text\": \"Put the bundles in a row: stock = [{\\\"name\\\": \\\"Rice\\\", ...}, {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Sugar\\\", ...}]. Now each item's facts travel together - deleting one item deletes all its facts at once, and nothing can desynchronize. To find something, loop and check labels: \\\"if item[\\\"name\\\"] == \\\"Tea\\\":\\\" - the same loop-plus-decision pattern from Day 3, now with labeled data. This shape - a list of labeled records - is how almost all real data looks, including the JSON you will save on Day 6.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"example\": {\n      \"language\": \"python\",\n      \"filename\": \"stockbook.py\",\n      \"run\": \"Run python stockbook.py from the starter directory.\",\n      \"context\": \"Amina's stock book: three items as labeled bundles in a list. Add flour, find tea, count the book. Predict the two printed lines before you run.\",\n      \"code\": \"stock = [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Rice\\\", \\\"price\\\": 250, \\\"qty\\\": 40},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Sugar\\\", \\\"price\\\": 180, \\\"qty\\\": 90},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Tea\\\", \\\"price\\\": 950, \\\"qty\\\": 25},\\n]\\n\\nstock.append({\\\"name\\\": \\\"Flour\\\", \\\"price\\\": 120, \\\"qty\\\": 60})\\n\\nfor item in stock:\\n    if item[\\\"name\\\"] == \\\"Tea\\\":\\n        print(f\\\"Found Tea: Rs {item['price']}, qty {item['qty']}\\\")\\n\\nprint(f\\\"Items in the stock book: {len(stock)}\\\")\",\n      \"output\": \"Found Tea: Rs 950, qty 25\\nItems in the stock book: 4\",\n      \"walkthrough\": \"stock starts as a list of three dictionaries - three bundles in a row. append() adds a fourth bundle at the end; the list is now four long, and nothing else had to change. The loop takes each bundle in turn and checks its \\\"name\\\" label; only Tea's bundle matches, so only one line prints. len(stock) counts the bundles: 4, because the appended flour counts. Predict first: if you searched for \\\"Bread\\\" instead, nothing would print - the loop would simply find no match, which is also the answer to \\\"how do I know it is missing?\\\"\"\n    },\n    \"exercises\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Remove an item\",\n        \"task\": \"Remove Sugar by building a new list with only the items you want to keep. Predict the length, then print it.\",\n        \"hint\": \"Loop over stock; append each item whose name is not \\\"Sugar\\\" to a new list.\",\n        \"check\": \"Length is 3 after removal; rice, tea, and flour remain.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Change a price by label\",\n        \"task\": \"Raise Tea's price to 1000: loop, match the name, assign item[\\\"price\\\"] = 1000. Print the book after.\",\n        \"hint\": \"Loop, match the name, assign to the label.\",\n        \"check\": \"Tea shows Rs 1000; every other item is unchanged.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Count the stock value\",\n        \"task\": \"Loop over the book and add up price times qty for every bundle. Predict the total first.\",\n        \"hint\": \"total = 0 before the loop; inside: total = total + item[\\\"price\\\"] * item[\\\"qty\\\"].\",\n        \"check\": \"Rs 57150 (10000 + 16200 + 23750 + 7200), matching your prediction.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"mistakes\": [\n      \"Using item[\\\"Price\\\"] instead of item[\\\"price\\\"] - labels are case-sensitive; KeyError means the label does not exist.\",\n      \"Forgetting that positions start at 0 - stock[3] on a 3-item list is an error.\",\n      \"Keeping three parallel lists instead of bundles - one deletion desynchronizes everything.\"\n    ],\n    \"fde\": \"Almost every junior ticket touches labeled data: \\\"the API returns a list of orders - show each customer's total.\\\" That API data arrives as JSON, which looks exactly like Python dictionaries in a list. Reading item[\\\"price\\\"] today is reading real API responses next week. And the parallel-lists bug - rice getting sugar's price - is a real production bug juniors get asked to fix. Bundles prevent it by construction.\",\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"Dukaan v0.5 - the stock book\",\n      \"brief\": \"Amina's shop now has four items and she keeps mixing up the three parallel lists. Rebuild v0.5: the stock becomes one list of dictionaries. Every feature - the list, the bill, the alerts, the report - must work on the new shape. Deleting an item must be one operation, not three.\",\n      \"deliverables\": [\n        \"dukaan.py stores stock as a list of dicts with name, price, qty labels.\",\n        \"All v0.5 features work unchanged on the new shape.\",\n        \"New: remove an item by name in one operation; search prints one item's full record.\",\n        \"A note: one real mistake the old parallel lists could cause, in your own words.\"\n      ],\n      \"milestones\": [\n        {\n          \"title\": \"Convert the shape\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Replace the three lists with one list of dicts.\",\n            \"Update every loop and function to use labels.\",\n            \"Run the full program - output must match v0.5.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"Identical output to v0.5 for the same data.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"title\": \"Add and remove\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Add \\\"add item\\\" and \\\"remove item\\\" operations by name.\",\n            \"Remove Sugar; confirm rice and tea are untouched.\",\n            \"Re-add Sugar with new values.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"Removal is one operation and cannot desynchronize.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"title\": \"Search and report\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Add search: type a name, print that item's record.\",\n            \"Handle a name that is not in the book with a clear message.\",\n            \"Print the total stock value in the morning report.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"Unknown names get \\\"not found\\\", not a crash.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"acceptance\": [\n        \"Stock is a single list of dicts; no parallel lists remain.\",\n        \"All v0.5 features produce identical output on the new shape.\",\n        \"Removing an item by name is one operation and leaves other items intact.\",\n        \"Searching an unknown name prints a clear \\\"not found\\\" message.\",\n        \"You can explain why bundles beat parallel lists, with a concrete failure example.\"\n      ],\n      \"tests\": [\n        [\n          \"Same data as v0.5\",\n          \"Bill, alerts, and report match v0.5 exactly\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Remove \\\"Sugar\\\", then print the book\",\n          \"Two items remain; rice and tea unchanged\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Search \\\"Bread\\\"\",\n          \"Clear \\\"not found\\\" message, no crash\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Add \\\"Flour\\\" (120, 60), then count\",\n          \"Book length is 4\"\n        ]\n      ],\n      \"extensions\": [\n        \"Print the book sorted by price, cheapest first, without changing the stored order.\",\n        \"Refuse to add an item with a negative quantity, with a clear message.\"\n      ],\n      \"review\": [\n        \"What breaks in the parallel-lists version when you delete the second item but forget one list?\",\n        \"Why is item[\\\"price\\\"] clearer than prices[1] to a stranger reading your code?\",\n        \"Where else in real software have you seen labeled bundles? (Hint: Day 9's APIs.)\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"references\": [\n      [\n        \"Python: data structures\",\n        \"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html\"\n      ]\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 6,\n    \"title\": \"Saving the shop\",\n    \"intro\": \"Everything Dukaan knows lives in the program's memory. Close the program and it forgets - every morning Amina retypes the stock. Worse: one mistyped quantity crashes the whole bill. Today Dukaan v0.6 gets two upgrades: files give it a memory that survives closing, and try/except keeps it standing when input is bad.\",\n    \"objectives\": [\n      \"Save a Python data structure to a file and load it back with the json module.\",\n      \"Use try/except to handle missing files and bad input without crashing.\",\n      \"Explain why JSON is the format programs use to share structured data.\"\n    ],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Files remember after the program ends\",\n        \"text\": \"Variables die when the program stops. A file survives. open(\\\"stock.json\\\", \\\"w\\\") opens the file for writing; json.dump(stock, f) translates the list of dictionaries into text and writes it. Later, open(\\\"stock.json\\\") with json.load(f) translates the text back into Python values. JSON (JavaScript Object Notation - a boring name for a useful idea) is text shaped exactly like Python lists and dicts, which is why the round trip is so smooth. \\\"with\\\" closes the file for you, even if something goes wrong inside the block.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Errors are messages, not the end\",\n        \"text\": \"int(\\\"three\\\") fails. A missing file fails. In Day 2 that meant a crash; today it means a decision. try: says \\\"attempt this\\\"; except ValueError: says \\\"if that specific failure happens, do this instead.\\\" The program keeps running - it prints a helpful message, uses a safe default, and carries on. Catch specific errors (ValueError, FileNotFoundError), not everything: hiding all errors also hides your own bugs, and then nothing can be fixed.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"JSON: the text format programs share\",\n        \"text\": \"json.dump translates your list of dictionaries into text; json.load rebuilds it. The text is not Python - it is JSON, a format almost every language reads. That is why it matters beyond this lesson: on Day 9 you will read JSON from a real supplier API, and the file you save today is the same shape as the data that API sends. Keep the stock book to lists, dicts, text, and numbers - JSON cannot hold everything Python can.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"example\": {\n      \"language\": \"python\",\n      \"filename\": \"save.py\",\n      \"run\": \"Run python save.py from the starter directory. It creates and deletes its own demo file.\",\n      \"context\": \"Amina's stock book, saved to a file and loaded back. The file might not exist yet - the program must not crash. Read the code, predict the exact output, then run it. Then, as an experiment: comment out the save block and run again - predict which branch runs.\",\n      \"code\": \"import json\\nimport os\\n\\nFILENAME = \\\"dukaan_stock.json\\\"\\n\\nstock = [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Rice\\\", \\\"price\\\": 250, \\\"qty\\\": 40},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Sugar\\\", \\\"price\\\": 180, \\\"qty\\\": 90},\\n]\\n\\nwith open(FILENAME, \\\"w\\\") as f:\\n    json.dump(stock, f)\\nprint(\\\"Saved the stock book.\\\")\\n\\ntry:\\n    with open(FILENAME) as f:\\n        loaded = json.load(f)\\n    print(f\\\"Loaded {len(loaded)} items: {loaded[0]['name']} and {loaded[1]['name']}\\\")\\nexcept FileNotFoundError:\\n    print(\\\"No saved file yet - starting fresh.\\\")\\n    loaded = []\\n\\ntyped = \\\"three\\\"\\ntry:\\n    qty = int(typed)\\nexcept ValueError:\\n    print(f\\\"'{typed}' is not a number - using 0.\\\")\\n    qty = 0\\nprint(f\\\"Quantity accepted: {qty}\\\")\\n\\nos.remove(FILENAME)\\nprint(\\\"Demo file cleaned up.\\\")\",\n      \"output\": \"Saved the stock book.\\nLoaded 2 items: Rice and Sugar\\n'three' is not a number - using 0.\\nQuantity accepted: 0\\nDemo file cleaned up.\",\n      \"walkthrough\": \"json.dump translates the stock list into text and writes it to dukaan_stock.json. The try block then reads it back: the file exists, so json.load rebuilds the list and the \\\"Loaded 2 items\\\" line prints - the except branch is skipped. Next, int(\\\"three\\\") raises ValueError; the except catches it, prints the friendly message, and qty becomes 0 instead of crashing. Finally the demo deletes its own file so nothing is left behind. Now the experiment: comment out the save block and run again. This time open() raises FileNotFoundError, and you will see the \\\"starting fresh\\\" branch run. That branch is what v1.0 uses on every first launch.\"\n    },\n    \"exercises\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Watch the missing-file branch\",\n        \"task\": \"Comment out the save block (the \\\"with open ... 'w'\\\" part) and run. Predict what prints before you run.\",\n        \"hint\": \"Without the save, the file does not exist when the load runs.\",\n        \"check\": \"\\\"No saved file yet - starting fresh.\\\" prints, and the program finishes normally.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Corrupt the file\",\n        \"task\": \"In a scratch file, run only the load part after writing the text \\\"hello\\\" into dukaan_stock.json by hand. What error appears? Describe it in your own words.\",\n        \"hint\": \"json.load on non-JSON text raises json.JSONDecodeError.\",\n        \"check\": \"You can name the error and explain that the file's text was not valid JSON.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Round-trip your stock book\",\n        \"task\": \"Build your 3-item stock book (a list of dicts), save it with json.dump, load it back, and print loaded == stock_book.\",\n        \"hint\": \"== on two lists compares every value inside them.\",\n        \"check\": \"It prints True - the round trip lost nothing.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"mistakes\": [\n      \"Catching every error with a bare \\\"except:\\\" - it hides your real bugs along with the expected failures.\",\n      \"Mixing up the file modes: json.dump needs the file opened for writing, json.load for reading.\",\n      \"Saving values JSON cannot hold and being surprised on load - keep the stock book to lists, dicts, text, and numbers.\"\n    ],\n    \"fde\": \"Two rules every junior learns the hard way. One: users type anything - \\\"three\\\", \\\"-5\\\", an empty line. A program that crashes on bad input is a bug report waiting to happen, and \\\"handle invalid input gracefully\\\" is one of the most common junior tickets there is. Two: data loss is the unforgivable bug. Losing Amina's stock book would be worse than any crash. Save carefully, load defensively, and never let a missing file become a lost morning.\",\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"Dukaan v0.6 - memory that survives\",\n      \"brief\": \"Dukaan forgets everything when it closes - every morning Amina retypes the stock. Build v0.6: on start, load the stock book from dukaan_stock.json (or start fresh with a clear message if there is no file); after every change, save; and no bad input may ever crash it - \\\"three\\\", \\\"-5\\\", and empty input all get a friendly message.\",\n      \"deliverables\": [\n        \"dukaan.py loads the stock book from dukaan_stock.json on start, or starts fresh with a message.\",\n        \"Adding an item saves the file immediately.\",\n        \"A test note: the bad inputs you tried and what the program printed.\"\n      ],\n      \"milestones\": [\n        {\n          \"title\": \"Save the stock book\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"After adding an item, json.dump the list to dukaan_stock.json.\",\n            \"Run twice and open the file - it holds your items.\",\n            \"Confirm the file is plain text you can read.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"The file exists after the run and contains the items as JSON text.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"title\": \"Load it back\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"On start, try to load the file; on FileNotFoundError start with a fresh list and a message.\",\n            \"Add an item, close, reopen - the item is still there.\",\n            \"Corrupt the file (write \\\"hello\\\" into it) and run - handle it without a traceback.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"Stock survives a restart; a missing or corrupt file never crashes.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"title\": \"Unbreakable input\",\n          \"steps\": [\n            \"Wrap every int(input(...)) in try/except ValueError.\",\n            \"Test with \\\"three\\\", \\\"-5\\\", and empty input.\",\n            \"Negative or zero quantities get a message, not a crash.\"\n          ],\n          \"check\": \"Every bad input prints a message; the program keeps running.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"acceptance\": [\n        \"Stock added in one run is present after closing and reopening.\",\n        \"First launch with no save file starts fresh with a clear message.\",\n        \"A corrupt save file produces a message and a fresh start, not a traceback.\",\n        \"Typing \\\"three\\\", \\\"-5\\\", or nothing at a quantity prompt shows a message, not a crash.\",\n        \"You can explain what try/except does in one sentence, using the missing file as the example.\"\n      ],\n      \"tests\": [\n        [\n          \"Add Rice, close, reopen\",\n          \"Rice is listed\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Delete dukaan_stock.json, run\",\n          \"\\\"No saved file yet - starting fresh.\\\"\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Write \\\"hello\\\" into dukaan_stock.json, run\",\n          \"Friendly message, fresh start, no traceback\"\n        ],\n        [\n          \"Type \\\"three\\\" at a quantity prompt\",\n          \"Message shown; program continues\"\n        ]\n      ],\n      \"extensions\": [\n        \"Keep a backup: before overwriting, copy the old file to dukaan_stock.bak.\",\n        \"Pretty-print the JSON with indent=2 so Amina can read the file herself.\"\n      ],\n      \"review\": [\n        \"Why is the load wrapped in try/except but the save usually is not?\",\n        \"What Python values can JSON not hold? What would break if the stock book used them?\",\n        \"Amina deletes the file by accident. What does she lose, and what would you change so she loses less?\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"references\": [\n      [\n        \"Python: reading and writing files\",\n        \"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/inputoutput.html#reading-and-writing-files\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Python: errors and exceptions\",\n        \"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/errors.html\"\n      ]\n    ]\n  }\n]")
